#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86357526-2549-528e-bfa7-d59ddbeec572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102164} {:answer/id #uuid "a927aad3-2d84-56e3-8432-a24f56f0c218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102165} {:answer/id #uuid "353321ee-0aa5-573a-8577-1de1370d3029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102166} {:answer/id #uuid "3f5934d3-bbc1-521e-b0f9-2e9b899b4898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102167} {:answer/id #uuid "5e45d239-017f-5f86-bbdc-b52f0f992554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "353321ee-0aa5-573a-8577-1de1370d3029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102166} :answer-field/id #uuid "287553fe-4e24-5a0c-9d12-a33a2f963403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7aed349f-e0e0-5f67-bd76-7726539edfdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161146" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/assets/365c6e6ebc861b9d8276773d5afe39b971096f0de6f07fced2c5a2ea9e035554.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{r}^{3}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbb35f73-000b-5dd0-9af7-8405ff0c229c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369663} {:answer/id #uuid "71ab6b1f-7537-56c7-a91d-cc35ada3305d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369664} {:answer/id #uuid "c30a68c3-d552-5ff5-af08-b6265e7505ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369665} {:answer/id #uuid "50ddf406-d08f-5e58-8cb5-9875a83a3660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369666} {:answer/id #uuid "cef52014-d237-5950-a5cd-c78abe0cfbe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef52014-d237-5950-a5cd-c78abe0cfbe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369667} :answer-field/id #uuid "f304aab7-8cf3-5f8d-9a8a-5c49bf869a77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9649fbd0-1b6c-528b-8da4-a1be1daa517f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161291" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/assets/9795c04bf6d4bfa407a7b9176cd5c4b97b3d89bfcc6fff17d1d78190047b3a54.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88283c52-40b6-56b7-af29-e18d67ef32e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369670} {:answer/id #uuid "63151eb9-bf57-55b5-8fdc-afac770aad35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369671} {:answer/id #uuid "463544d0-14db-5630-bc6d-9a6a8fc9d023" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369672} {:answer/id #uuid "ef52d9b5-c02a-5137-9a54-c47613c02fe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369673} {:answer/id #uuid "f651c958-8371-533f-92a3-b2fd0adf1911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f651c958-8371-533f-92a3-b2fd0adf1911" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369674} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc1f8a23-5e32-5981-abd8-f80e77b735c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f99d977-1723-5815-b4ef-c6c2935d34e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161311" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071063/assets/5ba2dbc746b9fe8b36202d31bbeb8193ffb9e747d6ed4c2e2f0f994499f88e8e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-x+\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the second quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf710525-dec7-5721-a80f-5d4503682156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369677} {:answer/id #uuid "d1365b6c-5a7c-5af3-a69a-ae31cfe14916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369678} {:answer/id #uuid "c609278a-276b-5ecc-a894-996454276e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369679} {:answer/id #uuid "08f53e71-fdd5-5471-8f97-4c036a182a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369680} {:answer/id #uuid "10c00b51-03f3-59bd-8273-6450ce7282c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10c00b51-03f3-59bd-8273-6450ce7282c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369681} :answer-field/id #uuid "76379828-47fb-5348-a0cb-635e9e78d497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47133f58-913f-5380-a818-45651da6032d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161341" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4(x+y)\\,\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4xy+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[2{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b609b55-3239-5299-8526-71550310509e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369684} {:answer/id #uuid "be746125-7e97-5a88-aac0-474bd70f0d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369685} {:answer/id #uuid "8d02d57b-c747-5fd3-93ce-6a90ad7c5082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369686} {:answer/id #uuid "68c24785-84d4-5191-a56c-a23320ef29a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369687} {:answer/id #uuid "3a00e1e0-5c1f-5ab3-90cb-6edda8f6ee9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d02d57b-c747-5fd3-93ce-6a90ad7c5082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369686} :answer-field/id #uuid "54078bfe-041a-5e3e-a8ac-f41779b7d52c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e724fa5-e67d-5f9a-9ecd-ea5d2768a0b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161353" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $-x\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1+x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1+x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}(1+x+y)^{2}}\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-x}^{0}\\,\\frac{1}{(1+x+y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[-\\frac{1}{1+x+y}]}_{y=-x}^{y=0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(-\\frac{1}{1+x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+x]_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
